" "basis" 1451 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-43920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=2t+\\sqrt{t}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5dc5188d8fdf6aafffa23f9aa3023e6b5623ad9e1db3086f8368ea1909ef020" "basis" 1451 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-43920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+\\sqrt{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+4t\\sqrt{t}+t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{8{t}^{2}\\sqrt{t}}{5}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{5}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{103}{30}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{\\frac{103}{30}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5dc5188d8fdf6aafffa23f9aa3023e6b5623ad9e1db3086f8368ea1909ef020" "basis" 1451 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-439