ccessful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-97410" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩=2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-97410" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $‖v‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot (2\\cdot 2)+3\\cdot 3+2\\cdot (4\\cdot 4)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8+9+32} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-97410" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "80fec36a7cafbb2fd5ad8479fee505def4c237401c1797d0842fb782d46a9cee"} {"question" "q-97410" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327