orked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $p(t)=4-{t}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=3,\\,p(0)=4,\\,p(-1)=3.\n$$\n\nComputing $‖p(t)‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{p(1)p(1)+p(0)p(0)+p(-1)p(-1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{[p(1)]^{2}+[p(0)]^{2}+[p(-1)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+16+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-97422" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "80fec36a7cafbb2fd5ad8479fee505def4c237401c1797d0842fb782d46a9cee"} {"question" "q-97422" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-97422" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4"] ["math" "4\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "\\sqrt{10}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{34}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-97422" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{34}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-42734" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[\\pi ,2\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[\\pi ,2\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-42734" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\,{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{t}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{4}){|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\pi }{4})-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =(\\pi +0)-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we made use of the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡t=\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖f(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-42734" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "80fec36a7cafbb2fd5ad8479fee505def4c237401c1797d0842fb782d46a9cee"} {"question" "q-42734" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-42734" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{\\pi }"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{3\\pi }{2}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\pi }"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-42734" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-97715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,4]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,4],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{4}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=\\sqrt{t}+1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-97715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(\\sqrt{t}+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(t+2\\sqrt{t}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{4t\\sqrt{t}}{3}+t)|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =(8+\\frac{32}{3}+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{68}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{\\frac{68}{3}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-97715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "80fec36a7cafbb2fd5ad8479fee505def4c237401c1797d0842fb782d46a9cee"} {"question" "q-97715" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-97715" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\sqrt{23}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{32}{3}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{34}{3}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{56}{3}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{68}{3}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-97715" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{\\frac{68}{3}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-43920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=2t+\\sqrt{t}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-43920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+\\sqrt{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+4t\\sqrt{t}+t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{8{t}^{2}\\sqrt{t}}{5}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{5}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{103}{30}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{\\frac{103}{30}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-43920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "80fec36a7cafbb2fd5ad8479fee505def4c237401c1797d0842fb782d46a9cee"} {"question" "q-43920" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-43920" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{103}{30}"] ["math" "\\frac{33}{10}"] ["math" "\\frac{33}{5}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{103}{30}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{33}{5}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-43920" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{\\frac{103}{30}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-43921" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-43921" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\,{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{t}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{4}){|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi }{4})-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi )}{4}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we made use of the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡t=\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖f(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-43921" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "80fec36a7cafbb2fd5ad8479fee505def4c237401c1797d0842fb782d46a9cee"} {"question" "q-43921" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-43921" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\sqrt{2\\pi }"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\pi +1}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\pi +\\frac{1}{2}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\pi }"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-43921" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{\\pi }"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-141170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[-1,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-1,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=1-t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-141170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({t}^{2}-2t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-{t}^{2}+t)|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-1+1)-(\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3}-(-1)^{2}+(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{3}}=2\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-141170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "80fec36a7cafbb2fd5ad8479fee505def4c237401c1797d0842fb782d46a9cee"} {"question" "q-141170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-141170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}"] ["math" "\\frac{8}{3}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{3}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-141170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-97725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩=3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \n\nNormalize the  vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-97725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨v,v⟩ & =3\\cdot (2\\cdot 2)+(-1)\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot (1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =12+1+3 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{16}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $v$, we divide $v$ by its norm $‖v‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{‖v‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-97725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "80fec36a7cafbb2fd5ad8479fee505def4c237401c1797d0842fb782d46a9cee"} {"question" "q-97725" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-97725" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-97725" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-42739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNormalize the vector $f(t)=t-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-42739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{(t-1)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,({t}^{2}-2t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-{t}^{2}+t)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-1+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $f(t),$ we divide $f(t)$ by its norm $‖f(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\frac{f(t)}{‖f(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{t-1}{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(t-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-42739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "80fec36a7cafbb2fd5ad8479fee505def4c237401c1797d0842fb782d46a9cee"} {"question" "q-42739" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-42739" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4(t-1)"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}(t-1)"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}(t-1)"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}(t-1)"] ["math" "t-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-42739" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{3}(t-1)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-97726" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩=2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \n\nNormalize the  vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-97726" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨v,v⟩ & =2\\cdot (3\\cdot 3)+2\\cdot (2\\cdot 2)+(-3)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =18+8+9 \\\\  & =35.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{35}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $v$, we divide $v$ by its norm $‖v‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{‖v‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{35}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{35}}{35}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-97726" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "80fec36a7cafbb2fd5ad8479fee505def4c237401c1797d0842fb782d46a9cee"} {"question" "q-97726" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-97726" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{22}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{22}}{22}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{35}}{35}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{7}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-97726" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{35}}{35}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-42741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V={R}_{1}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $1,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(1)y(1)+x(2)y(2).\n$$\n\nNormalize the  vector  $p(t)=2+t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-42741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nSince $p(t)=2+t,$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=3,\\,p(2)=4.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),p(t)⟩ & =[p(1)]^{2}+[p(2)]^{2} \\\\  & ={3}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\  & =9+16 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{25}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $p(t)$, we divide $p(t)$ by its norm $‖p(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\frac{p(t)}{‖p(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+t}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2+t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-42741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "80fec36a7cafbb2fd5ad8479fee505def4c237401c1797d0842fb782d46a9cee"} {"question" "q-42741" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-42741" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{5}(2+t)"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(2+t)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{50}(2+t)"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}(2+t)"] ["math" "\\sqrt{5}(2+t)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-42741" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{5}(2+t)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-141174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \n\nNormalize the  vector  $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-141174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨v,v⟩ & =2\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 1\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =4+3+1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $v$, we divide $v$ by its norm $‖v‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{‖v‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-141174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "80fec36a7cafbb2fd5ad8479fee505def4c237401c1797d0842fb782d46a9cee"} {"question" "q-141174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-141174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "q-141174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd4a90ae5f6ab36a3b374df6981c2a45ef51e563e038cbe0be0faecba6ad905c"} {"question" "e-5908" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An inner product on $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ is defined as $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle =  2x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 3x_3 y_3.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/assets/1e710ca7b3b7bea626de36181a30869b0f114a4497843c6b6ea8b8966a91d208.png) under this inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/example-5908.json" "file_sha256" "97d5c13f4824cfdcff3f77e1090b119e4b0c9c2629945e870dd798aeb284d0e9"} {"question" "e-5908" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $\\mathbf v$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot (3\\cdot 3)+2\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (1\\cdot 1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18+4+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/example-5908.json" "file_sha256" "97d5c13f4824cfdcff3f77e1090b119e4b0c9c2629945e870dd798aeb284d0e9"} {"question" "e-5548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n\\langle {x}(t), {y}(t) \\rangle = x(1)y(1) + x(2)y(2) + x(3)y(3).\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector  $p(t) = 1 - t^2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/example-5548.json" "file_sha256" "caa3644ef81a2ef1187c15d824c40cb22952aae1a8648cc1bbae24943353ad6e"} {"question" "e-5548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${p}(t) = 1-t^2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=0,\\qquad \np(2)= -3,\\qquad \np(3)=-8.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\|p(t)\\|$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{p(1)p(1)+p(2)p(2)+p(3)p(3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{[p(1)]^{2}+[p(2)]^{2}+[p(3)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+9+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{73}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/example-5548.json" "file_sha256" "caa3644ef81a2ef1187c15d824c40cb22952aae1a8648cc1bbae24943353ad6e"} {"question" "e-5549" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,1\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,1\\right],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)= 2t-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/example-5549.json" "file_sha256" "e5d17f0c3379426501e26d38a9b0d312cfbc85e221f128621f588429dec17864"} {"question" "e-5549" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\langle  f(t),  f(t) \\rangle}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}-4t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}-2{t}^{2}+t)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}-2+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{3}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/example-5549.json" "file_sha256" "e5d17f0c3379426501e26d38a9b0d312cfbc85e221f128621f588429dec17864"} {"question" "e-5550" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,\\pi\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,\\pi\\right],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nNormalize the vector $f(t)= 3t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/example-5550.json" "file_sha256" "dfbb85f02ee49bfd473f49ddbc76e1c9f8546f1c89ef9daa398ae5970b591375"} {"question" "e-5550" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\langle  f(t),  f(t) \\rangle}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{(3t)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }9{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{t}^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =3{\\pi }^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\| f(t) \\|  = \\sqrt{3\\pi^3} = \\pi \\sqrt{3\\pi}.\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $f(t),$ we divide $f(t)$ by its norm $\\| f(t) \\| \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\frac{f(t)}{‖f(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi \\sqrt{3\\pi }}\\cdot 3t \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\sqrt{3}}{\\pi \\sqrt{\\pi }}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/example-5550.json" "file_sha256" "dfbb85f02ee49bfd473f49ddbc76e1c9f8546f1c89ef9daa398ae5970b591375"}] "usage" {"q-97422" [] "q-97413" [] "e-5908" [] "q-97420" [] "e-5548" [] "e-5549" [] "q-43920" [] "q-97725" [] "e-5550" [] "q-42734" [] "q-43908" [] "q-42726" [] "q-141170" [] "q-97726" [] "q-42741" []} "basis" 1848 "retraction